Input

Flags:
      --state TEXT              Read this text instead of stdin. Use - to read stdin.
  -i, --input FORMAT            One of text, json, jsonl, lines, csv, tsv or request.
                                Default: text.
      --map EXPR                Select the content to evaluate with a jq expression.
      --delimiter CHAR          Set the field separator -i csv reads and -o csv writes:
                                auto, which needs -i csv and writes the one it detects,
                                or one character. Defaults to a comma.
      --image NAME=PATH         Put a PNG or JPEG in the state under NAME. Repeatable.
                                For one record stdin is read beside it only through
                                --state -, so text joins it from --state or --state -. A
                                stream reads its records from stdin and adds the image
                                to each. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --image-field NAME        Read a PNG or JPEG per record from the path this state
                                key holds. Streaming only, repeatable. Under -i lines
                                the line is the path. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --id EXPR                 Name each record on every output line with the string or
                                number this jq expression returns. Streaming only. It
                                runs one record at a time as the input is read, so keep
                                it cheap, such as a field lookup. -V lists the cap on
                                its length.
      --skip-blank              Drop blank lines with no output line. Streaming only.

How they work together:
  Text and JSON read one state. JSONL, lines, CSV and TSV stream records from stdin.
  Request input sends API shaped bodies as written. --state supplies text instead of
  stdin, and --state - explicitly reads stdin, so --state - <FILE reads a file. --map
  runs a jq expression on each original record and sends its result as the state. Map
  only the text a person would read. Object keys come out sorted. The result may nest
  max-map-depth levels deep and encode to max-line-bytes, as version lists. The original
  record remains available for merged output.

  --id runs one record at a time as input is read. Its result must be a string or
  number. Keep it cheap, such as a field lookup. version lists max-id-bytes.
  --skip-blank drops blank lines without emitting a result. The default field separator
  is ','. --delimiter auto needs CSV input, detects its separator and uses it for CSV
  output. An explicit delimiter takes one character. TSV uses tabs.

  --image NAME=PATH adds a PNG or JPEG under NAME. For one record, stdin is read beside
  an image only through --state -. Text can also join it through --state. Images cannot
  be mixed with request input. Streams read stdin and add the fixed image to every
  record. --image-field reads a PNG or JPEG path from a state key per record. Under
  lines input the line itself is the path. Image support is experimental on the TypeSafe
  side. See version for image, file, token and model limits.

